{:edb/db-before-t 1729 :edb/db-after-t 1730 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535042 50 #inst "2026-10-08T15:34:07.424Z" 13194139535042 true] [17592186157327 1020 #edb/ref 17592186328414 13194139535042 true] [17592186157327 1020 #edb/ref 17592186328421 13194139535042 true] [17592186157328 1020 #edb/ref 17592186328390 13194139535042 true] [17592186157329 1020 #edb/ref 17592186328397 13194139535042 true] [17592186157329 1020 #edb/ref 17592186328404 13194139535042 true] [17592186157329 1020 #edb/ref 17592186328407 13194139535042 true] [17592186157329 1020 #edb/ref 17592186328411 13194139535042 true] [17592186157892 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535042 true] [17592186157892 1100 "Simplify the integral ${\\int }_{-7}^{7}3{x}^{6}\\,\\text{d}x$ using symmetry." 13194139535042 true] [17592186157892 1100 "Simplify the integral $∫_{-7}^{7}3x^{6}dx$ using symmetry." 13194139535042 false] [17592186157892 1145 "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any even function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ is symmetric across the $y$-axis, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=2{\\int }_{0}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)=3{x}^{6}$ is an even function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =3(-x)^{6} \\\\  & =3{x}^{6} \\\\  & =f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)=3{x}^{6}$ is an even function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-7}^{7}3{x}^{6}\\,\\text{d}x=2{\\int }_{0}^{7}3{x}^{6}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" 13194139535042 true] [17592186157902 1017 #edb/ref 17592186156664 13194139535042 false] [17592186157902 1017 #edb/ref 17592186328428 13194139535042 true] [17592186157902 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535042 true] [17592186157902 1100 "Which of the following integrals evaluates to zero due to symmetry?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-2}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-2}^{4}4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x$" 13194139535042 true] [17592186157902 1100 "Which of the following integrals evaluates to zero due to symmetry?\n\n1. $∫_{-2}^{2}\\sin^{2} xdx$\n2. $∫_{-3}^{3}\\tan (2x)dx$\n3. $∫_{-2}^{4}4\\tan xdx$" 13194139535042 false] [17592186157902 1145 "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to $0$ due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)={sin}^{2}⁡x$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & ={sin}^{2}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{2} \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & \\ne -f(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2(-x)) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x) \\\\  & =-g(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)=4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ is an odd function, but integral III is not of the form ${\\int }_{-a}^{a}h(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ since the upper and lower limits are not opposites. Therefore, we cannot use symmetry to simplify the integral.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139535042 true] [17592186328390 1017 #edb/ref 17592186328391 13194139535042 true] [17592186328390 1089 "q-115278" 13194139535042 true] [17592186328390 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535042 true] [17592186328390 1100 "Which of the following integrals evaluates to zero due to symmetry?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt[3]{x}\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-2}^{4}\\frac{7{x}^{3}}{1+2{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x$" 13194139535042 true] [17592186328390 1113 #uuid "3fb283e6-c950-59d4-b793-9a73d1c9314e" 13194139535042 true] [17592186328390 1145 "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to $0$ due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x}$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =\\sqrt[3]{-x} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt[3]{x} \\\\  & =-f(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt[3]{x}\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & \\ne -g(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)=\\frac{7{x}^{3}}{1+2{x}^{4}}$ is an odd function, but integral III is not of the form ${\\int }_{-a}^{a}h(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ since the upper and lower limits are not opposites. Therefore, we cannot use symmetry to simplify the integral.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139535042 true] [17592186328391 1000 #edb/ref 17592186328392 13194139535042 true] [17592186328391 1000 #edb/ref 17592186328393 13194139535042 true] [17592186328391 1000 #edb/ref 17592186328394 13194139535042 true] [17592186328391 1000 #edb/ref 17592186328395 13194139535042 true] [17592186328391 1000 #edb/ref 17592186328396 13194139535042 true] [17592186328391 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535042 true] [17592186328391 1041 "selection" 13194139535042 true] [17592186328391 1104 #edb/ref 17592186328394 13194139535042 true] [17592186328391 1110 #uuid "8aa056